---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
---

<CaseLayout
  headline="Designing Customer Insights and extending it with Upsell Opportunities."
  intro={[
    'Customer Insights began as an underused Cisco dashboard. I led the research and redesign around the questions account teams asked while preparing for customer meetings. Its user base grew 40% within three months of the redesign.',
    'As more people used Customer Insights, Cisco teams responsible for upgrades and buying programs asked to add their offers to the dashboard. I designed a framework for adding those offers one at a time and reusing data already available in Customer Insights.',
  ]}
  stakeholder="Cisco Ready"
  skills={['Product Discovery', 'Information Architecture', 'Platform Design']}
  year="2020-21 (two phases)"
  role="Sole Product Designer"
  teamLabel="Team"
  hero="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-extension-flow.webm"
  heroVideo
  heroPoster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-extension-flow.jpg"
  heroCaption="Sole Product Designer · Two phases. I redesigned Customer Insights, then designed a framework for adding offers from other Cisco teams."
  theme="cisco"
  previousHref="/work/hitachi-energy/"
  nextHref="/work/panw-ai/"
>
  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Researching Account Strategy</h2>
    <p>The existing page gave account managers a broad overview of a customer. To prepare a strategy, they still had to gather information from several internal tools and build their own pivot tables.</p>
    <p>I focused the interviews on participants’ two most recent customer meetings because meeting preparation was one of the few times account teams gathered all the information they needed to develop a strategy.</p>
    <p>I interviewed 12 participants across three countries. Most managers described a short set of questions they tried to answer while developing a customer strategy, and their pivot tables showed how they combined information that the dashboard kept separate.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="asymmetric-grid cisco-evidence-grid" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/previous-dashboard.png"
      width={3000}
      height={1605}
      alt="Previous Customer Insights dashboard"
      caption="The previous dashboard summarized the account. Users gathered the information needed for further analysis from other tools."
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/participant-pivot-analysis.png"
      width={1709}
      height={3000}
      alt="Participant-created customer analysis pivot tables"
      caption="Account teams’ pivot tables showed the customer comparisons they needed to make."
    />
  </div>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/strategy-sensemaking.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Framework connecting customer strategy questions to accessible data"
    caption="I mapped the questions from the interviews to their data sources and checked which information Customer Insights could access."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing Reusable Insight Modules</h2>
    <p>I designed visualizations to answer the questions account managers considered while developing a customer strategy.</p>
    <p>I organized those visualizations into repeatable modules so users could move from a summary into the underlying data. One module used expandable table rows, and the same interaction could be reused across different datasets.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/visualization-ideation.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1129}
    alt="Board of data visualization concepts"
    caption="Visualization studies tested how the same customer question could move from a quick signal to a detailed analysis."
    class="cisco-visualization-board"
  />

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/video/data-table-module.webm"
    poster="/assets/cisco/posters/data-table-module.jpg"
    width={1920}
    height={666}
    alt="Reusable data table module expanding and collapsing rows"
    caption="The reusable table module moved between collapsed summaries and expanded rows without leaving the page."
    class="cisco-data-table-module"
    video
    controls
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Testing Dashboard Concepts</h2>
    <p>To decide where users should begin, I translated one product question into two opposing principles. Concept 01 gave users a catalog of visualizations and the freedom to decide what mattered. Concept 02 curated metrics against predefined benchmarks so the dashboard could recommend where to look first.</p>
    <p>Participants preferred the guided starting point because it helped them choose a next step before reading a chart. Customer differences made benchmark colors unreliable, so the final design kept the tile hierarchy and removed benchmark-based status colors.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="media-grid cisco-concept-grid" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/exploratory-concept.png"
      width={3000}
      height={2009}
      alt="Exploratory chart-led dashboard concept"
      caption="Concept 01 gave users cataloged visualizations and the freedom to decide what mattered."
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/guided-concept.png"
      width={3000}
      height={2009}
      alt="Benchmark-guided metric tile dashboard concept"
      caption="Concept 02 used predefined benchmarks to recommend a starting point."
    />
  </div>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/video/customer-insights-flow.webm"
    poster="/assets/cisco/posters/customer-insights-flow.jpg"
    width={1920}
    height={1080}
    alt="Customer Insights dashboard workflow"
    caption="The final dashboard organized the account around six questions. Each module opened with a headline measure, followed by charts and tables."
    video
    controls
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column case-transition" data-reveal>
    <h2>Impact of Phase One</h2>
    <p>Over the three months after the redesign, the number of users grew by 40%.</p>
    <p>That growth showed program teams where account managers were spending their time, so they began asking to add upgrades and buying-program offers to Customer Insights. Each offer required its own data validation and backend integration, and we could only add one at a time. I needed a repeatable way to add more offers later.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="platform-evolution" data-reveal>
    <div class="platform-host">
      <div class="platform-core">
        <span>CORE PRODUCT</span>
        <strong>Customer Insights</strong>
        <p>Customer information organized around the questions account teams asked.</p>
        <div class="platform-ports" aria-label="Platform rules">
          <span>Placement</span><span>Customer data</span><span>Offer language</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="platform-contributions" aria-label="Replaceable contributed capabilities">
        <div><span>PROGRAM TEAM 01</span><strong>Upgrade offer</strong><p>Replaceable</p></div>
        <div><span>PROGRAM TEAM 02</span><strong>Buying program</strong><p>Replaceable</p></div>
        <div><span>PROGRAM TEAM 03</span><strong>Service offer</strong><p>Replaceable</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Customer Insights could add or update offers from different program teams through the same framework.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/extension-constraints.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1144}
    alt="Framework for scaling contributed opportunity recommendations"
    caption="Offers arrived in phases, could change over time, and always referenced an existing customer KPI."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Testing Opportunity Placement</h2>
    <p>I tested panels, lists, overlays, and inline modules with account-team participants. People often overlooked the panel and list when they used internal program names, even when the panel occupied a quarter of the screen.</p>
    <p>Internal program names did not explain why an offer mattered. I rewrote each offer around the action an account manager could take and the potential value for that customer.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="media-grid cisco-extension-tests" data-reveal>
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-panel-test.webm"
      poster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-panel-test.jpg"
      width={1790}
      height={1280}
      alt="Opportunity panel usability concept"
      caption="Panel test: the large panel was easy to see, but participants still overlooked offers labeled with internal program names."
      video
      controls
    />
    <Figure
      src="/assets/cisco/video/opportunity-list-test.webm"
      poster="/assets/cisco/posters/opportunity-list-test.jpg"
      width={1790}
      height={1280}
      alt="Opportunity list usability concept"
      caption="List test: offers remained independent from the existing tiles, leaving room for more offers later."
      video
      controls
    />
  </div>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/opportunity-card-copy.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Opportunity card before and after copy refinement"
    caption="“Upsell to NCD” became “Upgrade to Next Calendar Day,” paired with the potential value for that customer."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Connecting Opportunities to Customer Data</h2>
    <p>I reviewed spreadsheets from the program teams and found that every offer reused a subset of data already shown in Customer Insights.</p>
    <p>I treated each offer as a lens on that data. Selecting one filtered or expanded the existing charts and tables.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/data-lens-model.png"
    width={3000}
    height={853}
    alt="Several opportunities reusing subsets of Customer Insights data"
    caption="Each offer filtered a different subset of data already available in Customer Insights."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Impact of Phase Two</h2>
    <p>Customer Insights became the only Cisco platform where account teams could analyze current customer data and identify growth opportunities in one place. At that point, Customer Insights supported four offers.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/cisco/opportunity-collection.png"
    width={3000}
    height={1687}
    alt="Four Upsell Opportunity views available in Customer Insights"
    caption="Customer Insights supported four Upsell Opportunities at this stage."
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>What I learned</h2>
    <p><strong>Phase one: A clear research and design roadmap helped me win stakeholder support.</strong></p>
    <p>The roadmap showed why the research was necessary, which case studies informed the approach, and how long the work would take.</p>
    <p><strong>Phase two: I had to understand what motivated account managers to act.</strong></p>
    <p>They paid attention when an offer showed how it could help them reach quarterly or annual goals. A larger, brighter module alone had not produced that response.</p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
